and you have an x-ray,

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:03.880
you should be looking at this tactic.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:04.920
There was the move 94.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:06.800
Yeah, there was the move 94,

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:07.680
and then we would have been able

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:08.840
to take the rook in the corner.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:12.240
Again, that's a moot point now because white's castled.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.760
Now c5 becomes a genuine positional threat

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:17.640
because it can be supported with b4,

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:20.480
and then our bishop is genuinely gonna be buried

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:21.960
under the pile of rubble.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:23.680
And I'll show this after the game,

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:26.600
so don't worry too much if you're not able to visualize this.

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:29.120
That's what the post-game analysis is for.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:32.520
Let's complete our development with the move knight bd7.

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:33.920
I really don't see why not.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:35.880
I really don't see why not.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:37.000
And we've completed our development.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.680
We have a nice position.

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:41.280
Our opponent has also handled things very decently.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.080
I actually think I had a better move, though.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:45.320
I'm bummed.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:46.480
I rushed it.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:50.000
There was a move here that went after the bishop pair,

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.000
which some of you have suggested, knight h5,

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.320
which I think we should have played in this position.

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:55.960
But knight bd7 is also fine.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:58.940
Nothing wrong with that move.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:01.440
What's our mid-game plan for this setup?

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.000
Well, it really depends almost entirely

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:05.440
on what our opponent plays next

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.160
because our opponent, I think,

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:08.800
has two main ways of approaching this.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.120
He can make these sort of general improving moves

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:14.440
like rook ac1, or maybe rook f to d1.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:17.200
Then we will embark on one concept.

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.320
But our opponent could also play this more aggressively

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:23.000
and play a move like e4 and try to expand in the center.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.400
Our opponent could also discharge the tension

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:26.520
with c takes b5.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:27.920
Then we would have to decide

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:29.360
which pawn to recapture with.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.440
So I'm not thinking too much right now

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.080
about what our next move is gonna be.

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:34.800
We're just kind of waiting for our opponent

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:37.080
to reveal what his plan is.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:38.560
So this is a misconception

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:40.640
that when your opponent is thinking,

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.880
you have to know what you're gonna play next.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:44.640
You have to have a clear plan in mind.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.240
B.S., in many positions,

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:49.480
your plan depends entirely on what your opponent does,

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:51.760
and you should spend your opponent's turn

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:53.860
just sort of making general observations

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:56.580
that could help you accelerate the process

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:57.500
of looking for a move.

00:12:57.980 --> 00:12:59.260
So what sorts of observations are we making?

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:01.620
Well, I'm noticing that the bishop on b7

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:03.900
can be cleared up with the move c5.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.340
The diagonal can be opened.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:07.060
So that's something that we should keep in mind.

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:09.900
Now, c5 does blunder the b5 pawn,

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.740
but we could potentially take the pawn on c4

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:14.100
and then follow up with c5.

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:17.300
This is a very typical idea when you've got this setup.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.940
I'm also keeping an eye on the possibility of e5.

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:22.820
The queen on d2 and the queen on d8 are in a standoff.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:24.900
So there could be certain situations

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:26.540
where e5 is possible,

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:28.580
even though there are more defenders on that square

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.420
than there are attackers from black side,

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:33.620
because maybe the queen on d2 becomes loose in some line.

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:35.660
So I'm making that mental note to myself.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:37.340
Of course, we could also play rook e8

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:38.540
in order to prepare e5.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:41.940
Our opponent makes a general waiting move, h3.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:43.020
Okay.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:44.660
So the way that I see it, we have two approaches.

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.540
We can continue playing the waiting game

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:49.700
with a move like rook e8, potentially preparing e5.

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:52.500
But I pointed out that preparing c5

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.460
is more in the spirit of this position,

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:56.220
and that's what we're going to go for.

00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:00.220
So again, if we play the immediate c5,

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:01.620
we blunder the pawn on b5.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:02.540
Everybody should see that.

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:03.540
White has three attackers,

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:05.860
and we would only have one pawn defending.

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:07.860
So before playing c5,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.060
we need to discharge the tension with bc.

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:11.940
And you might look at this and say,

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:14.380
well, don't you leave the a6 pawn isolated?

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:17.060
Isn't this pawn now a big weakness?

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:18.180
Can't argue with that.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.020
It is a weakness.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:19.860
It's isolated,

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:21.620
but it's not the most important pawn in the world,

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:23.860
and it's very hard for white to attack.

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:25.900
This is what I think a lot of people miss

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:27.140
when they think about weaknesses.

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:29.500
They think, okay, a weakness is bad.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.300
Yes, but you also have to ask yourself

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:35.300
how easy or hard is it for your opponent

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:37.060
to actually attack that weakness?

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:39.340
The pawn is defended nicely by the bishop and the rook.

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:41.460
White can't attack it even if he tried.

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:43.100
And even if we lose that pawn,

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:44.660
it's really not the end of the world.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:49.180
So weakening a corner pawn is rarely a reason

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:51.180
like not to do something.

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.180
There must be other drawbacks

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:56.100
for you to be dissuaded from playing that one.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:57.580
So what does the move c5 do?

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:59.220
Well, it opens up the bishop.

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:00.260
Why is that important?

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:02.620
We're already threatening to take the knight on f3

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:04.660
and permanently cripple white's pawn structure.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:06.420
That's a huge positional threat.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:08.220
d5 is correct.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:09.860
Our opponent closes down the center,

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:12.180
leading to a sort of Benoni structure,

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.460
which is defined by the pawn on c5

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:16.540
and white's pawn on d5.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:17.660
This is a type of structure

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:20.700
which arises very frequently from the modern Benoni.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:24.020
I'll show how that happens after the game.

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:25.980
We can't waste time here

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:28.100
because if we give white a couple of tempi,

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:29.540
what is white going to do?

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:32.100
Well, white's gonna play e4 and expand in the center.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:34.700
White will be having the positional threat of bishop h6.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:37.020
We've already talked about the importance

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:38.860
of maintaining our dark squared bishop here.

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:41.180
That's the soul of our position.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:42.660
Plus the bishop on b7 is like,

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:44.180
hey, what are you doing to me?

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.780
Now I'm staring at this pawn.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:48.420
But this pawn on d5 is also a target

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:50.060
and we can use that pawn

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:53.980
to assemble our pieces nicely on the queen side, I think.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:55.580
There is an obvious move in this position

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:56.820
that I think we should flick in

00:15:56.820 --> 00:15:58.300
because it doesn't do any harm.

00:15:58.300 --> 00:15:59.740
Yeah, I'm talking about knight b6.

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:00.940
Let's hit the bishop.

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:04.540
Let's increase and intensify the pressure on the d5 pawn.

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:06.740
And let's force this bishop to commit

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:09.060
to a bad square, such as a2,

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.740
where it's really not doing anything

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.260
other than defending this pawn.

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:14.660
There's certain moves that you could really play

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:16.900
without knowing what your follow-up is

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:19.780
because clearly this move doesn't do any harm.

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:22.420
So right now we're making it one move at a time.

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:24.020
We're waiting for our opponent's response

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:26.500
and then we're calibrating our subsequent play

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:29.460
based on what our opponent does.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:31.500
Yeah, well, it would be nice if we could play e6,

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:32.500
which is common in the Benoni.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:33.340
We can't do that.

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:35.420
If we play e6, we blunder the pawn

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:37.740
and you could say, oh, but then we can take on f3,

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:40.260
but you're gonna end up giving two of your pawns away.

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:41.860
That's not a worthy price to pay

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:43.580
because white's king is still pretty safe,

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:46.140
even if white has to play gf.

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:47.380
So e6, I really don't like.

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:50.420
Also, it weakens the d6 pawn, as Akila points out.

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:51.940
Okay, so if you play the Benoni,

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:54.260
and I'm sure at least one person in the chat

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:56.020
plays the modern Benoni,

00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:58.220
the move that I kind of wanna play here,

00:16:58.220 --> 00:16:59.420
and I have two ideas,

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:02.020
so I actually will need to think a little bit.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:03.900
The move, one move that I wanna play

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:05.660
is incredibly typical in the Benoni,

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:07.100
and that's the move c4,

00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:10.060
slicing off the connection between the bishop and the pawn

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:12.620
and vacating the c5 square,

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:14.900
which we can use as an outpost

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:16.300
actually for the f6 knight.

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:19.700
We could play c4 and then knight fd7 and knight c5,

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.220
putting pressure on various weak squares

00:17:22.220 --> 00:17:23.500
in white's position.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:24.740
So I really like the look of c4.

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:25.900
Let's go for it.

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:27.860
Again, a low-risk move.

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:30.180
We push the pawn, we blot the bishop,

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:32.940
we attack d5, and we force white to play e4.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:36.220
Without the move e4, white's pawn would have been lost.

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:38.540
White has no other way to defend d5.

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:41.260
Falk asks, can't white play b4 here?

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:43.740
I mean, b4 is not a dangerous move.

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:46.940
It opens up the diagonal and it blunders the pawn on d5.

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:48.380
But we're not afraid of b4.

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:50.900
Now, white has to play e4.

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:52.100
Our opponent is taking a while here,

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:55.660
not really sure what, okay, e4, exactly, yes.

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:59.460
So now e6 is a more appealing move,

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.220
but it's still bad because the d6 pawn

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:03.540
becomes a serious weakness.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:07.020
As a result, in fact, we would lose that pawn, e6, d, e.

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:09.380
We could go for some tactics there with knight takes e4,

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:11.540
but I'm pretty skeptical about that

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:13.100
because we are gonna lose the d6 pawn

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:14.900
pretty quickly after that.

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:16.140
Although it has to be calculated,

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:18.900
it's actually not totally clear-cut

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.060
that e6 is a terrible move here.

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:24.500
So what is white's positional threat in this situation?

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:28.740
Once white plays e4, a positional threat has been created,

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:30.460
which we cannot prevent,

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.180
but we can take the sting out of it,

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:34.660
and hopefully some of you know this technique.

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:36.380
The threat is bishop h6.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:37.260
Why is that a threat?

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:39.580
Well, that forces the exchange of bishops.

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:40.940
Why does it force the exchange of bishops?

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:42.980
Because the only way to keep the bishops alive

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:45.180
is to duck with bishop h8,

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:47.500
but then we would be losing the exchange.

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:50.620
So how do we take the sting out of the move, bishop h6?

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:52.380
Well, we need to move the rook first

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:54.620
so that bishop h6 could be met with bishop h8.

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:57.620
This is a very, very standard technique

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:59.980
that is applied oftentimes in the dragon

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:01.980
and in other openings where you fianchetto the bishop

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:03.620
and where you have to keep that bishop.

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:05.740
The health of your position depends

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:08.060
on your ability to keep that bishop alive.

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:11.020
Now, our next few moves are probably going to be

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:13.340
knight fd7 and then knight c5,

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:15.500
and we're going to try to start accumulating the pressure

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:17.500
on white's center, and eventually,

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:20.060
I think the light-squared bishop can be reignited

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:21.980
if we're able to play the move e6.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:24.620
Queen e2, okay.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:28.460
So obviously, our opponent hits the pawn on c4.

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:29.980
That's fine.

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:31.820
That's not such a big deal.

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:34.700
In fact, I would even consider sacrificing that pawn

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:37.580
in order to generate and get the bishop pair.

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:39.420
We have a couple of ways to defend that pawn.

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:42.780
The move that I really like here is actually a5.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:44.620
I really like the look of a5.

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:46.460
What does a5 accomplish?

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:48.140
Who can tell me what that move accomplishes?

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:50.700
It grabs a little bit of space on the queen side,

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:52.620
but how does it prevent bishop takes c4?

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:53.740
Very nice.

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:55.180
Very nice.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:20:01.020
So a5, bishop takes c4, knight takes c4, queen takes c4.

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:03.260
We have bishop a6 at the end of the line

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:06.300
skewering the f1 rook and the queen on c4.

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:08.460
Of course, white can block with knight b5,

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:10.780
but then the e4 pawn is going to be lost.

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:13.180
Are there alternatives to that move?

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:15.580
Well, we could again consider the move e6.

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:19.500
e6 might actually be an even better move in this position.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:21.820
Now, what is it about e6 that I like?

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:24.060
Well, why was e6 bad previously?

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:26.380
It was bad because the d6 pawn would have been lost.

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:28.700
Here, the queen has moved away from d2,

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:30.460
so e6 is a lot more appealing.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:32.700
So let me calculate this for a moment.

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:35.020
e6, pawn takes, rook takes.

00:20:35.980 --> 00:20:37.260
White has knight g5.

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:40.700
We bring the rook back, bishop takes c4, d5.

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.540
Man, that looks so much fun.

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:44.140
You know what?

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:46.540
We're playing in the spirit of the Benoni,

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:47.980
and that's an aggressive opening,

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:50.460
an opening in which opening up the center

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.300
favors the side with the more active pieces.

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:54.700
What I love about the move e6

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.460
is also that it opens up the light script bishop,

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:58.140
and eventually we want to do that anyway.

00:20:58.140 --> 00:20:59.660
So I don't see a reason for us to wait.

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:02.780
The x-ray that the rook exerts on the queen

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:05.900
is yet another reason to play e6 specifically here.

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:08.300
Okay, our opponent takes immediately,

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:09.420
and so we respond.

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:14.140
Now, is this risky?

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:14.700
Absolutely.

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:16.540
This is a risky strategy for black.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:20.140
But look at how our pieces are breathing now.

00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:22.140
The bishop, the knight, the rook attack e4.

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:24.780
Our dark-squared bishop is intact and safe.

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:27.100
Are our pawns weak?

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:28.380
Yes, they're extremely weak.

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:31.340
That is the biggest flaw of our position.

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:32.780
So what that means is

00:21:32.780 --> 00:21:35.420
we have to conduct the initiative carefully.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:37.340
The more pieces get traded,

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:39.420
the more white repels our initiative,

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.380
the likelier it is that our active pieces

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:45.580
are canceled out by our incredibly weak pawns.

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:47.580
But bishop takes c4, I think, is a mistake.

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:49.340
How should we respond?

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:51.180
Should we play knight takes c4?

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:52.620
Well, no, because the rook is hanging.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:54.460
So should we meekly move the rook away?

00:21:54.460 --> 00:21:55.500
No, we have a better move.

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:56.860
Why did we put the knight on b6?

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:58.860
Well, we were defending the c4 pawn.

00:21:58.860 --> 00:22:00.060
We, of course, take the bishop.

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:02.060
And we do it without too much thought.

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:04.540
And then we play knight takes c4.

00:22:04.540 --> 00:22:05.660
Again, playing quickly

00:22:05.660 --> 00:22:07.500
because there's nothing to calculate here.

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:10.220
One, two defenders, one, two attackers.

00:22:10.220 --> 00:22:11.020
Look at our bishops

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:12.860
and how they're crisscrossing the board.

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:15.580
The bishop on f4 can also get in trouble.

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:17.340
If white plays knight takes knight,

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:19.420
we respond with rook takes knight,

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:21.100
forking the queen and the bishop.

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:24.140
Now, technically, the queen could drop back

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:25.500
to c1 in that position,

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:26.860
if you're able to visualize,

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:28.300
and protect the bishop.

00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:30.300
But then we could bring the other rook into the game.

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:31.820
And the queen could get into b6.

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:33.500
And this is why openings like the Benoni

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:35.260
and the Grunfeld are so much fun,

00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:37.820
because you learn proper piece coordination.

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:39.740
And you really begin to appreciate the value

00:22:40.300 --> 00:22:41.820
of having active pieces,

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:44.380
which sounds like a meaningless cliche.

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.220
But that's incredibly important

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:47.900
as a beginner and early intermediate.

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:51.900
Zazaz says, why not take with the bishop?

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:53.500
Well, because the bishop is a treasure.

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:55.340
It's a treasure because it attacks

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:56.140
the knight on f3.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:57.660
And in general, having the bishop pair

00:22:57.660 --> 00:22:58.940
in an open board is good.

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:01.020
So we don't want to give away

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:01.740
that bishop for free.

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:02.620
Bishop takes c4,

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:03.580
white would take with a knight,

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:05.740
or white would even have knight g5,

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:07.180
forking the rook and the bishop.

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:09.020
That would be an even bigger reason

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:10.620
not to take with the bishop here.

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:11.980
Knight to d4.

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:13.100
Okay, that's a good move.

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:14.060
It's a really good move.

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:16.940
But nothing that we can't deal with.

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:18.780
So what does this move do?

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:19.740
Well, it attacks the queen.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:20.620
It attacks the rook.

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:23.740
We cannot play a move like rook c8 here,

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:25.420
because white takes the rook anyway,

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:27.020
simultaneously hitting the queen.

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:28.700
So we do need to move the rook.

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:29.740
Bishop takes d4,

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:30.940
we're not even talking about,

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:31.900
because there's no way

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.580
that I'm giving this bishop away

00:23:33.580 --> 00:23:34.300
without a fight.

00:23:34.300 --> 00:23:35.420
Where should we go with our rook?

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:37.420
Well, we should just move it back to e8.

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:38.460
Why not to e7?

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:40.300
Because that walks into various tempo moves,

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:41.820
like knight d5 and knight c6.

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:44.220
And we also want to open up the diagonal,

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:46.940
keep that diagonal open for our queen.

00:23:48.220 --> 00:23:49.660
So I don't see a reason for us

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:51.900
not to bring the rook back to e8.

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:54.620
d5 didn't work.

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:55.900
d5, knight takes c6.

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:57.180
Same reason that rook c8 didn't work.

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:58.380
That's why I pointed out that line.

00:23:59.420 --> 00:24:01.260
d5, knight takes rook.

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:03.100
And that simultaneously hits the queen.

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:04.620
So then dc, knight takes d8.

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:05.100
And at the end,

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:06.300
you're going to be down an exchange.

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:10.540
So when you're considering a threat on your piece,

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:11.180
don't just say,

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:12.700
oh, he's attacking the rook

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:14.380
so I can counterattack their queen.

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:16.060
You also have to understand

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:18.220
what happens after the rook is captured.

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:20.220
Does that create any additional threats?

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:21.740
Does it attack your queen?

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:22.700
In this case, it does.

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:24.780
So you know that counterattacking

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:26.620
your opponent's queen isn't a good idea.

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:27.260
Knight d5.

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:29.500
I was a little bit worried about this move.

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:31.740
But now that I look at it a little bit closer,

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:33.020
I think it might be losing the game.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:35.420
Now, what is the first thing that I see

00:24:35.420 --> 00:24:36.540
when I look at the move, knight d5?

00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:39.180
Well, what I see is that both of our bishops

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:40.300
are making contact

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:41.820
with both of our opponent's knights.

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:45.340
This is a situation that I have talked about before.

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:48.460
It's a situation that makes it very likely

00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:49.580
that we can make a move

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:50.620
that will separate

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:52.860
one of our opponent's defenders

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:53.740
from their pieces.

00:24:53.740 --> 00:24:56.540
Because the queen is defending both of the knights.

00:24:56.540 --> 00:24:58.940
So logically, what type of move are we looking for?

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:01.020
Well, perhaps a move that attacks the queen.

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:03.180
Because perhaps the queen will not be able

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:04.540
to move to a square

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:06.620
where it can defend both knights at the same time.

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:09.100
Mathematically, there is no such square in existence.

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:11.340
Well, there's c5, but it's controlled.

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:12.940
So even without calculation,

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:15.020
you should be able to come up with rook c8

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:17.100
on the basis of your intuition alone.

00:25:17.980 --> 00:25:19.580
Now, the line doesn't end here.

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:21.900
The queen can move to a square

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:24.940
such that it attacks one of our minor pieces.

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:26.380
Now, the knight is protected by the rook,

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:27.900
so queen e2 is not effective.

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:31.020
Queen d3 blunders the knight on d5.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:33.180
Queen a2 blunders the knight on d4.

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:35.900
But there is the tricky move, queen b3.

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:38.220
What does queen b3 accomplish?

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:39.820
Well, it hits the bishop on b7.

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:42.140
So you might say, okay, well, queen b3,

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:43.100
let's take one knight

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:44.220
and then let's take the other knight.

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:46.700
But queen takes bishop, defends the other knight.

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:49.580
But don't stop calculating there.

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:51.980
Queen b3, bishop takes knight,

00:25:51.980 --> 00:25:53.820
queen takes knight, we have a winning move.

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:56.700
We actually have a winning move in this position as well.

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:59.100
I think there are two ways to win a piece.

00:25:59.900 --> 00:26:02.060
But even simpler is bishop takes d5,

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:04.700
queen takes d5, and rook c5 to hit the queen.

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:06.460
It's not a queen trap.

00:26:06.460 --> 00:26:07.980
The queen can drop back to a2,

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:09.820
it can drop to b7, doesn't matter,

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:11.660
because the knight on d4 is going to be lost.

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:14.380
You might say, well, what about bishop takes d5,

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:17.820
queen takes d5, rook c5, knight d4 to c6?

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:19.340
Well, then we just take white's queen.

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:21.260
Because once white takes our queen,

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:22.380
we just recapture the knight.

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:24.540
And in either case, we're up a minor piece.

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:26.460
So just double checking.

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:27.580
Yeah, I think we can take.

00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:29.980
So we give up our light squared bishop,

00:26:29.980 --> 00:26:31.260
but we do it for the greater good.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:33.420
And rook c5 wins the knight.

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:34.060
That's it.

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:35.980
That's why the queen is the worst defender.

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:36.780
Something I've been saying

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:38.140
since the start of the speedrun.

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:40.460
It's what's behind the rule,

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:42.940
which states that you should do things

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:44.540
with a piece of lower value.

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:46.540
The lower value the piece,

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:48.380
the better it is as a defender.

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:50.140
It didn't matter though.

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:51.580
I mean, white was losing anyway.

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:53.820
White was losing anyway.

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:55.900
Yeah, queen takes c6.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:57.420
At least it keeps the knight alive, right?

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:58.860
At least our opponent defended the knight.

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:00.240
GG.

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:01.900
But that's not disappointing

00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:03.020
because the game was over anyway.

00:27:03.020 --> 00:27:04.540
We were winning a full minor piece.

00:27:05.180 --> 00:27:06.140
There's a lot to talk about.

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:07.180
That was 24 moves,

00:27:07.180 --> 00:27:08.780
but there is a lot to unpack here.

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:11.580
And we're going to do a little bit of opening analysis,

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:13.180
because I will admit I'm not too familiar

00:27:13.180 --> 00:27:14.620
with how our opponent handled the opening.

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:15.740
I've never faced that before.

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:17.500
So we're going to see

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:20.460
if I was right to transition us

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:21.660
into a King's Indian position,

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:22.940
or whether it was possible

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:24.460
to still stick with the Grunfeld.

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:28.620
Okay, so d4, knight f6, c4, g6.

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:31.660
Previously, we faced a sort of Fianchetto structure,

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:33.020
a game that I still haven't analyzed,

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:34.220
but I will still put that out.

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:35.980
Knight f3.

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:37.580
So as I indicated,

00:27:37.580 --> 00:27:40.300
after knight c3, the Grunfeld is d5.

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:43.340
After c takes d5, knight takes d5,

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:45.340
white generally occupies the center,

00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:48.140
and it's very important that black has the move

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:48.940
knight takes knight,

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:50.940
which affords black a tempo

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:52.620
that is used to Fianchetto the bishop,

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:53.980
and then we play c5,

00:27:53.980 --> 00:27:55.660
and we chip away at white's center.

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:57.260
This is the mainline Grunfeld,

00:27:57.260 --> 00:27:58.380
and I'm sure that we're going to get

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:00.860
the mainline Grunfeld in subsequent games.

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:03.500
So I won't talk about the theory here right now.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.340
Let's focus instead on what our opponent did.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:06.700
So first things first,

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:09.020
why is d5 a bad move here?

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:10.940
It's because after cd,

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:12.700
well, queen takes d5 is just crap,

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:14.300
because you walk right into knight c3.

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:17.340
For knight takes d5,

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:18.300
white plays e4,

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:19.420
and knight takes knight.

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:20.060
Now wait a second,

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:21.820
there's no knight takes knight.

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:24.140
So our knight has to slink back

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:25.660
to a very passive square,

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:26.940
such as b6,

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:28.940
and white just gets excellent development.

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:31.500
White gets a tempo to prevent bishop g4,

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:33.340
and what you will find

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:34.940
is that in a position like this,

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:36.780
the standard Grunfeld move,

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:37.740
c5,

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:38.300
is terrible,

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:40.140
because white just takes the pawn

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:40.860
and hits the knight.

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:41.660
You should be able to see

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:42.940
that this is not the same

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:45.180
as c5 with the white pawn on c3.

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:47.180
Can you speak to us,

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:48.140
why not knight f6?

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:49.500
Well, knight f6,

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:50.140
knight c3,

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:50.700
and again,

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:51.660
after bishop g7,

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:53.580
your pieces are stumbling on each other.

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:55.900
This bishop is just staring at the knight,

00:28:55.900 --> 00:28:58.220
and white's center is left completely intact.

00:28:58.220 --> 00:28:59.580
It's unattackable.

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:01.660
Yeah, also e5 with tempo is annoying.

00:29:02.380 --> 00:29:05.020
So that is why here you play bishop g7.

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:07.420
If the moment white plays knight c3,

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:08.460
to play the Grunfeld,

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:09.260
you go d5,

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:09.660
and again,

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:11.500
if white takes it and plays e4,

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:14.940
then you're able to play knight takes c3 and c5.

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:17.900
And again,

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:20.220
we transpose to the main line Grunfeld.

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:22.620
Hopefully that makes sense.

00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:23.260
Now you might say,

00:29:23.260 --> 00:29:23.580
well,

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:25.580
why do we need to rush with d5?

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:26.460
I play castles.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:27.580
That's my main move.

00:29:27.580 --> 00:29:28.620
But after e4,

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:30.060
you get into the main line

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:31.580
of the King's Indian Defense,

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:34.220
which is characterized by pawns on e4,

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:34.620
d4,

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:35.100
and c4.

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:36.140
Little side note,

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:37.420
a lot of people get confused

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:38.860
between the Peirce or the Perk

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:40.220
and the King's Indian Defense.

00:29:40.220 --> 00:29:41.660
There's one big difference.

00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:42.220
In the Peirce,

00:29:42.220 --> 00:29:43.580
the pawn is on c2.

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:44.460
In the King's Indian,

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:45.500
the pawn is on c4.

00:29:45.500 --> 00:29:47.100
That literally is it,

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:47.340
right?

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:49.500
The Peirce is like the King's Indian

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:50.940
against one e4.

00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:53.100
So it's like playing the King's Indian,

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:54.220
but there's a big difference.

00:29:54.220 --> 00:29:55.500
Here the pawn is on c2.

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:58.700
Here the pawn is on c4.

00:29:58.700 --> 00:29:59.980
You can see otherwise the position

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:01.340
is exactly the same.

00:30:01.340 --> 00:30:02.300
I guess after knight f3,

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:03.020
bishop g7.

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:07.260
But this ostensibly small difference

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:09.740
actually makes a huge impact

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:11.420
on the development of the position.

00:30:11.420 --> 00:30:13.180
That's why the Peirce is a much worse

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:14.140
opening objectively,

00:30:14.140 --> 00:30:14.380
I think,

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:15.420
than the King's Indian.

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:16.860
The main reason is that

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:18.460
with the pawn still on c2,

00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:20.140
white's queenside is intact,

00:30:20.140 --> 00:30:21.500
which means that a lot of lines

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:22.940
in the Peirce involve

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:24.620
white castling queenside,

00:30:24.620 --> 00:30:25.740
just like in the Sicilian.

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:26.220
For instance,

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:27.340
bishop e3,

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:28.460
castles queen d2,

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:29.980
followed by queenside castle

00:30:29.980 --> 00:30:31.340
is one of the most dangerous weapons

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:32.300
against the Peirce.

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:33.500
Hopefully you can understand

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:35.340
that with a pawn already on c4,

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:38.060
the queenside is a much less safe place

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:39.100
for white's king.

00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:40.540
So that basically eliminates

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:42.940
a whole host of possible responses.

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:44.540
Also, the light squared bishop

00:30:44.540 --> 00:30:46.380
finds it harder in the King's Indian

00:30:46.380 --> 00:30:48.380
to develop to an active square.

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:50.300
Here the bishop can be developed to c4.

00:30:51.660 --> 00:30:53.340
Also, there's the Austrian attack,

00:30:53.340 --> 00:30:55.020
which is similar to the four pawns attack.

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:55.980
In the Austrian attack,

00:30:56.540 --> 00:30:57.580
white plays f4,

00:30:57.580 --> 00:30:59.260
one of the most dangerous systems

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:00.060
against the Peirce.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:02.540
Meanwhile, the four pawns attack

00:31:02.540 --> 00:31:03.900
against the King's Indian,

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:06.540
which is knight c3, bishop g7, e4, d6, f4

00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:08.220
is a dangerous line,

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:09.660
but it's not as dangerous

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:10.780
as the Austrian attack

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:13.580
because here white overextends his center.

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:15.420
White has four pawns in the center,

00:31:15.420 --> 00:31:17.020
hence the four pawn attack,

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:18.940
but black has very effective ways

00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:20.860
to chip away at that center

00:31:20.860 --> 00:31:22.780
with c5 and e5.

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:24.700
So anyways, I could talk about this forever.

00:31:24.700 --> 00:31:25.980
Let's focus on the game.

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:28.060
Knight f3, bishop g7,

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:30.220
and our opponent plays this odd move,

00:31:30.220 --> 00:31:30.940
bishop f4,

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:32.220
kind of some sort of weird

00:31:32.220 --> 00:31:33.820
London-y type setup.

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:34.940
I'm going to check the engine here

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:37.340
to see what Mr. Stockfish likes us to do,

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:38.380
which is always a good idea

00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:39.820
when you face an unfamiliar opening.

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:42.300
Okay, so I had a suspicion

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:43.500
that this was the best move.

00:31:44.140 --> 00:31:46.860
The engine does approve of castles and d6.

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:50.380
The move d5 here might seem very tempting

00:31:50.380 --> 00:31:52.780
because after c takes d5,

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:53.820
knight takes d5,

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:55.100
you hit the bishop with tempo,

00:31:55.100 --> 00:31:56.620
but who can remind me

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:58.620
why this is actually a lot less effective

00:31:58.620 --> 00:31:59.500
than it appears?

00:31:59.500 --> 00:32:00.460
The engine confirms this.

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:01.980
White is slightly better in this position.

00:32:02.620 --> 00:32:03.420
What does white do?

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:05.740
No, not bishop g5.

00:32:05.740 --> 00:32:07.020
Bishop g5 is innocuous.

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:08.140
Bishop not e3.

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:11.260
Not e3 because then we get the bishop pair,

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:12.940
and c5 wins a pawn

00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:14.460
because white can't take.

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:15.340
Bishop takes b2.

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:16.380
It's bishop e5.

00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:19.660
No, guys, g3, e3,

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:20.940
that just gives up the bishop pair,

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:21.900
and black is doing well.

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.300
Bishop e5 is the reason this is annoying.

00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:26.540
So always remember

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:27.900
you shouldn't give away

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:30.060
your Grunfeld or King's Indian bishop

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:32.620
without a proper reason to do so.

00:32:32.620 --> 00:32:34.700
Here, it's very hard to avoid the trade.

00:32:35.420 --> 00:32:37.340
Trading and white is simply better

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:38.300
because what are you going to

00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:39.580
attack white's center with?

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:41.100
Now, yeah, you can play knight f6,

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:42.380
but you've wasted a tempo.

00:32:42.380 --> 00:32:43.660
You've played knight takes d5,

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:44.540
back to f6.

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:46.700
You've given white tempo for development,

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:48.300
and black is slightly worse here.

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:48.940
In the Grunfeld,

00:32:48.940 --> 00:32:50.220
you don't want your knight to be on f6.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:52.860
You want the bishop to have a direct line of fire

00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:54.940
to the d4 pawn, okay?

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:57.100
So that is why I decided

00:32:57.100 --> 00:32:59.100
on a more flexible approach, sorry,

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:00.380
which is to castle,

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:02.860
delaying the movement of the d-pawn,

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:04.940
waiting for white to put their cards on the table.

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:08.220
But the engine actually likes the immediate c5.

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:09.580
This is the engine's preferred move.

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:12.060
And I pointed this out during the game.

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:13.180
c5 is very good

00:33:13.180 --> 00:33:15.020
because the b2 pawn is unprotected.

00:33:15.020 --> 00:33:15.900
And after d5,

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:18.780
the engine actually proposes

00:33:19.340 --> 00:33:22.380
a Benko Gambit-style idea.

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:24.700
Who can tell me what move that implies?

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:27.100
When I say a Benko Gambit-style idea,

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:28.620
if you know what the Benko Gambit is,

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:29.980
you'll know what move to play here.

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:32.060
Yeah, b5.

00:33:32.060 --> 00:33:33.260
Sacrificing the pawn

00:33:33.260 --> 00:33:36.300
in order to separate white's pawn chain,

00:33:37.100 --> 00:33:37.980
but more importantly,

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:39.100
open up the queen's side.

00:33:39.660 --> 00:33:41.420
And how do you follow up the move b5?

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:43.020
After c takes b5,

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:44.940
who can tell me what the typical follow-up is?

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:46.300
It's a6, yeah.

00:33:46.300 --> 00:33:47.740
Yeah, it's a6, right?

00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:49.740
So now you're trying to get rid of the b-pawn.

00:33:49.740 --> 00:33:50.860
Why are you trying to get rid of it?

00:33:50.860 --> 00:33:52.700
So that the queen can develop to b6

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:54.940
and have a direct line of fire to the b2 pawn.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:58.860
The classic Benko Gambit is to play c5,

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:01.180
which is the entrance to the modern Bononi.

00:34:01.180 --> 00:34:01.980
While we're at it,

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:03.180
let me show you what the Bononi is.

00:34:03.180 --> 00:34:04.860
The Bononi is the move e6 here.

00:34:05.420 --> 00:34:08.620
And hopefully you're able to see the similarity

00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:10.300
between this structure

00:34:10.300 --> 00:34:11.820
and the structure that we had in the game.

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:14.460
So in the game, look at the similarity.

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:16.300
It's exactly the modern Bononi structure

00:34:16.300 --> 00:34:17.580
with a pawn on d5.

00:34:17.580 --> 00:34:19.580
Here, it's the same structure.

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:21.500
White plays e4 and life goes on.

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:23.020
This is a very theoretical opening.

00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:23.900
I won't delve into it.

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:26.940
But the Benko Gambit is the immediate b5,

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:28.220
c takes b5 and a6,

00:34:28.220 --> 00:34:29.420
named after Pal Benko,

00:34:29.420 --> 00:34:30.700
Hungarian grandmaster

00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:31.660
who recently passed away.

00:34:31.660 --> 00:34:32.620
He was U.S. champion.

00:34:33.660 --> 00:34:35.740
And this is still a very reputable opening.

00:34:35.740 --> 00:34:37.660
White is better with best play.

00:34:37.660 --> 00:34:39.660
But if you want an aggressive line against d4,

00:34:39.660 --> 00:34:43.020
you should absolutely consider the Benko Gambit.

00:34:43.020 --> 00:34:44.060
So in any case,

00:34:44.060 --> 00:34:45.900
if we compare this version

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:48.940
to the version in this position,

00:34:48.940 --> 00:34:50.620
this is an improved version for black

00:34:50.620 --> 00:34:52.540
because we've already fianchetted our bishop,

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:55.260
and the bishop on f4 is incredibly awkward.

00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:57.580
It's neither here nor there,

00:34:57.580 --> 00:35:00.220
and the b2 pawn is even more vulnerable.

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:02.140
So how is the game likely to continue?

00:35:02.140 --> 00:35:04.300
Well, b takes a6 is likely.

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:06.780
And now the engine gives the immediate queen b6

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:08.540
because the a6 pawn is not going anywhere.

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:09.900
We don't need to recapture it immediately.

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:10.940
And guess what?

00:35:10.940 --> 00:35:14.380
White has a very hard time to defend the b2 pawn.

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:17.580
If white plays b3, this is already winning.

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:18.220
How is it winning?

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:19.500
Well, there's knight takes d5.

00:35:20.300 --> 00:35:21.980
Oh, and this is brilliant.

00:35:21.980 --> 00:35:23.820
Oh my gosh, this is an amazing line.

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:25.260
Okay, watch this.

00:35:25.260 --> 00:35:28.140
So knight takes d5 should make sense to everybody, right?

00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:29.420
But here's the catch.

00:35:29.420 --> 00:35:31.900
After queen takes d5, it looks like black has blundered.

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:35.340
Because if we take the rook, white takes our rook,

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:37.020
and you can't trap the queen here.

00:35:37.020 --> 00:35:38.540
You don't have bishop b7.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:40.860
You're actually losing another minor piece.

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:43.580
But there is a ridiculous computer move in this position

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:44.620
which wins the game for black.

00:35:44.620 --> 00:35:46.460
I'll give people a chance to find it.

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:47.260
It's really hard.

00:35:47.260 --> 00:35:48.540
I didn't see it at all.

00:35:48.540 --> 00:35:50.460
I mean, once it's played, you understand the reason.

00:35:51.660 --> 00:35:54.140
Yeah, in Russia, the Benko is also called the Volga Gambit.

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:58.460
It's not queen a5 check because that doesn't do anything.

00:35:58.460 --> 00:35:59.980
What does queen a5 accomplish?

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:02.860
Still, if you take the rook, white takes the rook.

00:36:02.860 --> 00:36:04.220
It doesn't defend the rook.

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:07.580
It's not knight c6, although you're very close.

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:08.700
Knight c6 is good.

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:12.140
But here, white can intercept with knight e5.

00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:14.060
And the position is unclear.

00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:15.900
But you have the right square.

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:18.460
It's queen c6.

00:36:18.460 --> 00:36:19.260
People are getting it.

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:19.820
Very nice.

00:36:19.820 --> 00:36:21.260
It's queen c6.

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:26.540
It's not rook takes a6 because, again, white can intercept the bishop.

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:27.900
It's queen to c6.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:29.740
This is a double attack.

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:32.460
You're attacking the queen, and you're attacking the rook.

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:33.660
What is the purpose of that move?

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:36.140
Bishop b7, Frankfort Epoch blunders the bishop.

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:37.660
Don't forget about the pawn on a6.

00:36:38.380 --> 00:36:42.300
White has to trade queens because if white moves the queen, then we take the rook.

00:36:42.860 --> 00:36:45.820
So after queen c6, we play knight takes c6.

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:47.740
And look at what the knight on c6 does.

00:36:47.740 --> 00:36:50.540
It prevents white from sticking a knight on e5.

00:36:50.540 --> 00:36:52.460
So the rook is now trapped.

00:36:52.460 --> 00:36:56.060
If you say, oh, let's give up a knight to avoid losing the rook,

00:36:56.060 --> 00:36:58.300
you're actually going to lose the knight and lose the rook.

00:36:59.180 --> 00:37:03.500
If you say, okay, let's give up a knight like this, black plays knight takes e5,

00:37:03.500 --> 00:37:08.700
and white cannot prevent the further discovery of knight d3 or even knight back to c6.

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:10.540
You have to move your knight.

00:37:10.540 --> 00:37:14.060
If you move it to c3, bang, that's a fork.

00:37:14.940 --> 00:37:17.500
White has to take, and another fork on the rook.

00:37:17.500 --> 00:37:19.180
Queen c6 is ridiculous.

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:23.500
That's a GM-level move, but hopefully you're able to understand the point of that move.

00:37:23.500 --> 00:37:27.420
So obviously b3 is not forced, and you might say, well, what about queen b3?

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:29.980
Right, offering the exchange of queens.

00:37:30.540 --> 00:37:34.060
Well, here you play rook takes a6, another sexy move.

00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:38.620
And you say, I'm happy to trade queens, but first of all, I'm going to recapture your pawn.

00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:42.140
And this position is horrible for white because white is collapsing.

00:37:42.140 --> 00:37:44.460
White's losing a ton of pawns here.

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:47.980
d5 is hanging, b2 is hanging, and the bishop is going to be a monster.

00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:52.780
And if white takes the knight on b8, x-ray defending the bishop,

00:37:52.780 --> 00:37:56.380
then of course black has queen takes b3, and the rook is lost in the end.

00:37:56.380 --> 00:38:00.780
So these are dangerous lines, which shows you how strong the Benko Gambit can be.

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:04.700
White just doesn't have a good way of defending the b2 pawn.

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:07.980
The engine's move here is knight to c3, which I don't think a human would play.

00:38:08.940 --> 00:38:11.980
And after queen takes b2, apparently knight a4 equalizes,

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:15.100
but this still looks like a nasty position for white.

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:16.860
I would play this any day of the week with black.

00:38:17.740 --> 00:38:19.500
I don't see any other good ways to defend b2.

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:21.740
If you play queen c2, you drop this pawn,

00:38:21.740 --> 00:38:23.420
which is not a pawn you can afford to lose.

00:38:23.980 --> 00:38:27.180
Queen d2 walks into knight e4, and again you have queen takes b2 next.

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:30.220
So this is actually the best way to play.

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:35.180
You stick c5 in, and then you transition to a Benko Gambit-style treatment.

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:38.620
Of course white can play e3 and keep the pawn on d4,

00:38:39.340 --> 00:38:43.740
but I think cd4, ed4, and d5 is a very nice way to handle this position,

00:38:44.380 --> 00:38:48.780
because if white takes it, you get a beautiful isolated queen pawn structure

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:52.700
where you have control of the square in front of the IQP,

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:55.340
which as we know is almost always a good thing.

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:59.020
So c5 is going to be my official recommendation.

00:38:59.020 --> 00:38:59.820
Why didn't I do it?

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:01.420
Because I'm too obsessed with the King's Indian,

00:39:01.420 --> 00:39:06.540
and I kind of wanted to show the idea with d6, c6, a6, and b5,

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:09.740
just to add to people's collection of knowledge about this position.

00:39:10.460 --> 00:39:16.460
And I'll be honest, I didn't fully appreciate the power of the move b5.

00:39:16.460 --> 00:39:18.860
I saw this idea out of the corner of my eye,

00:39:18.860 --> 00:39:22.220
but I wasn't sure how much of an improved version

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:25.020
this is over the regular Benko Gambit,

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:27.100
and well, you know what the answer is now.

00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:28.140
Much improved.

00:39:28.140 --> 00:39:31.260
But it's not unheard of for me to play a Benko Gambit-style idea.

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:33.500
I remember a King's Indian game that I had some years ago,

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:36.940
where I won exactly in this fashion.

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:39.820
I transitioned to a Benko Gambit-style approach,

00:39:40.380 --> 00:39:43.260
and I mean, I crushed the guy because it was such an improved version.

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:43.900
Here's the game.

00:39:44.700 --> 00:39:48.380
This is from World Youth 2013, under 18.

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:50.860
My opponent was a pretty strong player.

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:52.860
So this started out as a Samish.

00:39:52.860 --> 00:39:54.300
Samish King's Indian with f3.

00:39:55.260 --> 00:40:00.220
And I developed a system where the knight from f6 goes back

00:40:00.220 --> 00:40:01.340
to open up the diagonal.

00:40:01.340 --> 00:40:02.300
You play c5.

00:40:03.020 --> 00:40:04.300
Now my opponent makes a mistake.

00:40:04.300 --> 00:40:06.540
He castles, allowing my knight to get out,

00:40:06.540 --> 00:40:10.300
and allowing me to secure the bishop pair, which I do.

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:13.180
But first, I decided to play what move?

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:17.180
If you guys have been paying attention, this move should be very natural.

00:40:17.180 --> 00:40:20.060
Especially because without the light squared bishop,

00:40:20.060 --> 00:40:22.460
our light squared bishop has incredible prospects.

00:40:22.460 --> 00:40:24.620
If you can get it on a6, the move is b5.

00:40:25.420 --> 00:40:26.700
White has to play cb.

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:30.540
Now we snap off the bishop, and we take back on b5.

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:33.820
My opponent takes back, and you should know what the next move is.

00:40:33.820 --> 00:40:34.860
It's bishop a6.

00:40:34.860 --> 00:40:37.100
Get the bishop out to the uncontested diagonal.

00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:37.660
Hit the knight.

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:39.420
My opponent drops it back.

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:42.220
Notice that a4 doesn't actually help,

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:45.100
because you can pile up the pressure on the knight in a million different ways.

00:40:45.100 --> 00:40:46.060
You go queen b6.

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:48.300
Okay, so that's not scary.

00:40:48.940 --> 00:40:49.980
My opponent goes back.

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:51.260
Here comes the queen.

00:40:51.260 --> 00:40:52.220
Here comes the rook.

00:40:52.220 --> 00:40:56.380
Just look at the pressure that my pieces are exerting, all of them.

00:40:56.940 --> 00:40:58.300
Rook fd1.

00:40:58.300 --> 00:41:01.260
Now bishop takes knight, which is a tactical move.

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:02.700
Positionally, it doesn't make sense.

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:04.140
But tactically, it does.

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:08.700
Who can spot the subsequent maneuver, which causes white's position to collapse?

00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:09.980
Why are we taking that knight?

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:12.220
Which piece can jump into the game now?

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:13.740
And where does it go?

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:15.900
What's the final destination for it?

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:17.740
It's not e5.

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:19.020
You don't want to close up the diagonal.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:19.820
It's knight e5.

00:41:20.460 --> 00:41:22.220
Oh yeah, I guess that's what you meant.

00:41:22.220 --> 00:41:25.660
Knight to c4 is a fork on the bishop and the pawn.

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:26.780
So the bishop has to go back.

00:41:26.780 --> 00:41:31.500
And now calm move, queen b4, threatening to take on b2.

00:41:32.060 --> 00:41:34.700
If white defends, then simply bishop takes knight.

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:37.020
That's the problem with having uncoordinated rooks,

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:38.860
is this is always going to lose.

00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:40.860
That's it.

00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:44.460
My opponent went a3, and I stick the queen right in that weak square.

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:47.580
And even in the endgame, white collapses because he's going to lose b2.

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:51.980
And once he loses b2, my c-pawn is going to decide the game because it's a passed pawn.

00:41:52.780 --> 00:41:53.900
Rook ab8.

00:41:53.900 --> 00:41:55.180
Finally, I take the pawn.

00:41:55.980 --> 00:41:56.940
The smoke clears.

00:41:56.940 --> 00:42:00.140
I stop white's pawn, and I just start pushing the c-pawn.

00:42:00.140 --> 00:42:00.860
The game is over.

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:02.140
c4, c3, c2.

00:42:02.700 --> 00:42:06.620
But the final detail after knight b4, rook takes a4.

00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:07.420
This is critical.

00:42:08.060 --> 00:42:11.980
If white takes the pawn, the rook swings over to c4, and the knight is lost

00:42:12.620 --> 00:42:16.620
because this is a perpetual pin, and just bishop b2 dislodges the rook.

00:42:18.060 --> 00:42:19.660
So my opponent went knight d3.

00:42:19.660 --> 00:42:20.460
I trade rooks.

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:22.700
And what's the final move of the game?

00:42:22.700 --> 00:42:26.700
It's obviously bishop b2 because if the knight takes, then black promotes.

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:28.620
Otherwise, we promote and win full piece.

00:42:28.620 --> 00:42:29.980
So my opponent resigned here.

00:42:29.980 --> 00:42:33.980
So a simple illustration of how powerful Benko-style ideas can be.

00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:37.340
Um, yeah, this was a very satisfying game.

00:42:38.380 --> 00:42:40.620
So anyways, let's go back to the game.

00:42:40.620 --> 00:42:41.980
We've spent a lot of time on the opening.

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:43.740
Let's move to the middle game.

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:47.900
But hopefully, this has been relatively illuminating.

00:42:47.900 --> 00:42:50.380
So we decided to play a King's Indian setup.

00:42:50.380 --> 00:42:52.380
So again, why didn't I play d5?

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:57.340
Well, again, it's because of this bishop e5 move, which I felt justifies the placement

00:42:57.340 --> 00:42:58.540
of the bishop on f4.

00:42:58.540 --> 00:43:00.060
I don't want to trade dark-squared bishops.

00:43:00.060 --> 00:43:04.780
Even if the computer says this is equal after knight f6, I felt like it wasn't really in

00:43:04.780 --> 00:43:05.980
the spirit of what I'm trying to do.

00:43:05.980 --> 00:43:07.660
So we played a King's Indian setup.

00:43:07.660 --> 00:43:08.860
Our opponent plays e3.

00:43:09.740 --> 00:43:11.260
We played c6.

00:43:11.260 --> 00:43:16.460
Yeah, bishop f4, castles, e3, d6, knight c3, c6.

00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:18.220
And white plays bishop e2.

00:43:18.220 --> 00:43:22.460
Not much to say about the way that our opponent is playing this setup.

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:26.300
And of course, a6 is the crux of our idea.

00:43:26.300 --> 00:43:31.020
So who can tell me what we would have done in response to a4?

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:34.380
This is very important because most people, I think, would play a4.

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:36.140
And you can't play b5.

00:43:36.140 --> 00:43:37.740
There are too many defenders on the square.

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:38.780
A rook is undefended.

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:43.420
So how do we exploit the hole on b4?

00:43:43.420 --> 00:43:45.020
Yeah, we play a5.

00:43:45.020 --> 00:43:46.300
We push the pawn twice.

00:43:46.300 --> 00:43:48.060
And then we play knight a6 and knight b4.

00:43:48.060 --> 00:43:49.180
And black is a great position.

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:51.900
Knight a6, let's say, h3.

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:54.380
We stick the knight on this beautiful outpost.

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:58.460
And then subsequently, our plan can be, for example, to prepare e5.

00:43:58.460 --> 00:44:00.620
How do we prepare e5 efficiently?

00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:03.420
Well, one way is to move the knight back to d7,

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:05.020
much like I did in that game.

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:06.700
This prepares e5 straight away

00:44:06.700 --> 00:44:09.580
because now you have two additional defenders on that square.

00:44:09.580 --> 00:44:10.940
Black has a perfectly good position.

00:44:11.500 --> 00:44:13.100
Black has a great King's Indian position,

00:44:13.100 --> 00:44:16.540
which you can largely play without knowing what the ideas are.

00:44:16.540 --> 00:44:19.660
You can play e5 and then deploy the queen to e7.

00:44:19.660 --> 00:44:22.380
Eventually, you're going to solve the problem of your light-squared bishop.

00:44:22.380 --> 00:44:24.620
BigSus asks, why not queen b6?

00:44:24.620 --> 00:44:27.180
Well, that's just a one-move threat, which doesn't do much.

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.100
It threatens the pawn, yes.

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:31.820
But here, white can defend with something like queen d2.

00:44:31.820 --> 00:44:35.020
And you've sort of committed the placement of your queen.

00:44:35.020 --> 00:44:36.220
You want your queen in the center

00:44:36.220 --> 00:44:40.620
because you want it to be able to support both sides of the board, right?

00:44:40.620 --> 00:44:44.220
Be very careful, very wary of one-move itis,

00:44:44.780 --> 00:44:47.500
which is a big enemy in these types of positions.

00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:52.140
I don't like c5 because it creates an equivalent hole

00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:52.860
on b5.

00:44:52.860 --> 00:44:56.940
Remember, that's why we played c6 to defend the b5 square.

00:44:56.940 --> 00:44:59.980
This just gives white an equivalent outpost.

00:44:59.980 --> 00:45:01.420
You know, why would you want to do that?

00:45:01.420 --> 00:45:03.820
Also, white can close up the center with d5.

00:45:03.820 --> 00:45:07.500
And with the bishop on f4, it's very, very hard for us to open the center

00:45:07.500 --> 00:45:09.500
because the d6 pawn is always going to be weak.

00:45:11.020 --> 00:45:13.980
So hopefully, that makes sense from a general standpoint.

00:45:13.980 --> 00:45:15.340
White played queen d2.

00:45:15.340 --> 00:45:17.580
Okay, we play b5.

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:21.580
So again, if white plays e4, we obviously play b4 and hit the knight

00:45:21.580 --> 00:45:22.220
and win the pawn.

00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:25.660
I think white is slightly better here according to the engine.

00:45:25.660 --> 00:45:27.260
So that's true.

00:45:27.260 --> 00:45:30.060
But the engine doesn't really appreciate king's Indian positions.

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:31.980
No, actually, black is doing great after b5.

00:45:31.980 --> 00:45:32.940
I just checked.

00:45:32.940 --> 00:45:35.420
But after a3, I think I made a small mistake.

00:45:36.620 --> 00:45:41.980
I feel like this would have been a great time for us to collect the bishop pair.

00:45:42.620 --> 00:45:45.740
And if you play the London system, which is unfortunate.

00:45:45.740 --> 00:45:48.060
Sorry, I had to refresh because it disconnected me.

00:45:48.060 --> 00:45:51.900
If you play the London system, you should know what this move is.

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:55.580
Now, in the London system, why does white play h3 early?

00:45:55.580 --> 00:45:57.580
What is the purpose of this move?

00:45:57.580 --> 00:45:59.900
What is it that you are taking the sting out of?

00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:04.860
And our opponent did this move, making it too late for us to initiate what I wanted to do.

00:46:04.860 --> 00:46:06.540
And so we played bishop b7.

00:46:06.540 --> 00:46:08.460
What was a suitable alternative here?

00:46:08.460 --> 00:46:10.220
And it is the top engine move I just checked.

00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:12.620
Knight h5, exactly.

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:14.140
You go after the bishop.

00:46:14.140 --> 00:46:15.740
The bishop can move to g5.

00:46:16.460 --> 00:46:18.060
But now we chase the bishop away.

00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:20.700
h6, bishop h4, and g5.

00:46:20.700 --> 00:46:22.300
Very important note.

00:46:22.300 --> 00:46:23.180
Knight takes g5.

00:46:23.180 --> 00:46:25.500
You might say, oh, it opens up a discovery on the bishop.

00:46:25.500 --> 00:46:26.700
But no, you take back.

00:46:26.700 --> 00:46:28.220
And again, you're attacking h4.

00:46:28.860 --> 00:46:30.540
And the knight can just drop back to f6.

00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:32.380
Insufficient compensation for the piece.

00:46:33.100 --> 00:46:34.780
Bishop g5 doesn't do anything.

00:46:34.780 --> 00:46:36.060
We just drop our knight back.

00:46:36.060 --> 00:46:37.980
So white has to go back to g3.

00:46:37.980 --> 00:46:40.700
Now, importantly, you don't have to rush knight takes g3.

00:46:40.700 --> 00:46:42.380
Where is this bishop going?

00:46:42.380 --> 00:46:43.340
It's not going anywhere.

00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:45.900
You can just let it sit on g3.

00:46:45.900 --> 00:46:49.500
Now, the moment white plays h3, then it's a good time to take the bishop.

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:51.260
But the engine likes bishop f5.

00:46:51.260 --> 00:46:55.660
Actually, it wants to develop the bishop to a different square, which is kind of interesting.

00:46:55.660 --> 00:47:01.660
And then eventually, you take the bishop on g3 when the time comes for that.

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:09.180
Yeah, bishop f5, for example, castles, bc, bishop c4, and again, knight d7 to prepare c5.

00:47:09.180 --> 00:47:11.340
But I love this position with the bishop pair,

00:47:11.340 --> 00:47:13.740
because the dark-squared bishop is going to be uncontested.

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:17.340
So I think knight h5 is a move that you should very often consider

00:47:17.900 --> 00:47:21.420
and occurs frequently in London-system-type positions.

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:23.340
Hopefully, that makes sense.

00:47:23.900 --> 00:47:25.820
Now, you might say, well, okay, but isn't...

00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:30.380
Yeah, as Akilah says, a lot of people are put off by this because you're weakening your kingside.

00:47:30.380 --> 00:47:31.980
And you are weakening your kingside.

00:47:31.980 --> 00:47:32.860
There's no doubt about that.

00:47:32.860 --> 00:47:36.460
I can probably show you some games where black does something like this,

00:47:36.460 --> 00:47:37.500
and then gets checkmated.

00:47:38.060 --> 00:47:39.820
Those games happen very often.

00:47:39.820 --> 00:47:43.020
But remember that white is probably going to castle kingside here.

00:47:43.020 --> 00:47:46.540
White can't really castle queenside, because look at the state of the queenside.

00:47:46.540 --> 00:47:47.660
We've got the pawn here.

00:47:47.660 --> 00:47:49.020
We want white to castle queenside.

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:50.140
We're going to checkmate him.

00:47:50.140 --> 00:47:54.700
And white, when he castles kingside, essentially relinquishes the ability

00:47:54.700 --> 00:47:56.780
to exploit the weak pawns.

00:47:56.780 --> 00:47:59.020
Now, you could say, well, could white stay in the center?

00:47:59.020 --> 00:48:01.740
Well, if white stays in the center, then the rooks are disconnected.

00:48:01.740 --> 00:48:04.380
And how do you propose that white exploits this weakness?

00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:08.780
It's much easier said than done, because knight takes g5 never really works.

00:48:09.340 --> 00:48:11.100
White can't get a queen to h7.

00:48:11.100 --> 00:48:13.260
And even if he could, the bishop defends that square.

00:48:13.260 --> 00:48:14.620
The other bishop defends h8.

00:48:14.620 --> 00:48:16.780
So don't worry about these positions.

00:48:16.780 --> 00:48:21.580
Just because the h file is open doesn't mean that it's automatically checkmated.

00:48:21.580 --> 00:48:26.140
You have to think concretely, OK, how can our opponent actually

00:48:26.140 --> 00:48:27.420
exploit the weakness of the king?

00:48:27.420 --> 00:48:31.100
So for instance, if we take this position, let's say white plays e4

00:48:31.660 --> 00:48:34.540
and chases the bishop away, then takes on g5.

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:37.820
Even if I gave you three moves, you couldn't checkmate me.

00:48:37.820 --> 00:48:42.700
Even if you put your queen on h4, these two mating squares are very safely protected.

00:48:42.700 --> 00:48:44.220
But what would I do here with black?

00:48:44.220 --> 00:48:48.140
I would play e5, opening up a channel between the queens.

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:51.980
Either white has to trade queens, or his center collapses entirely.

00:48:51.980 --> 00:48:57.420
So as you get to a more advanced level, 1800, you have to start thinking more concretely

00:48:57.420 --> 00:48:59.420
about the ramifications of your moves.

00:48:59.420 --> 00:49:02.380
And you shouldn't rule something out just because it has a drawback.

00:49:02.380 --> 00:49:05.180
Because at this level, things get more complex.

00:49:05.180 --> 00:49:09.020
At a lower level, games are decided by global blunder, right?

00:49:09.020 --> 00:49:11.900
And you can largely play moves that don't contain any drawbacks.

00:49:12.460 --> 00:49:14.540
At this higher level, you can't win a game.

00:49:14.540 --> 00:49:16.300
You can't make an omelette without breaking some eggs.

00:49:16.300 --> 00:49:21.100
So you have to start developing a concrete ability to compare the pros and cons

00:49:21.100 --> 00:49:22.780
of each move, which is incredibly hard.

00:49:23.580 --> 00:49:25.740
I wanted to show two other things before we move on.

00:49:25.740 --> 00:49:27.100
Two very instructive games.

00:49:27.100 --> 00:49:31.660
First, I wanted to do a search and to see if I could show you on the fly

00:49:31.660 --> 00:49:36.380
a game in which Black did this and then lost, crashed and burned.

00:49:36.380 --> 00:49:39.500
To show you that, yes, you know, these moves contain their drawbacks.

00:49:39.500 --> 00:49:40.700
And sometimes it's too much.

00:49:41.260 --> 00:49:42.380
But just give me a second.

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:44.540
Here's a game from 1995.

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:45.740
Nedarhanov is a GM.

00:49:46.460 --> 00:49:49.580
And this is a very similar position to what we just had.

00:49:49.580 --> 00:49:50.380
Guess what he plays?

00:49:50.380 --> 00:49:51.900
Oh, he plays g5 and knight h5.

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:55.100
Now, obviously, the problem with this move isn't that Black gets checkmated.

00:49:55.100 --> 00:49:57.020
White's already castled kingside.

00:49:57.020 --> 00:49:59.740
Who can tell me the blunder that Black has just committed?

00:49:59.740 --> 00:50:02.940
It's not like the biggest blunder in the world, but it lost him the game ultimately.

00:50:04.220 --> 00:50:06.060
What's the problem with knight h5 here?

00:50:06.700 --> 00:50:10.620
And don't just say knight takes g5, because, okay, knight g5, knight g3 is what he was

00:50:10.620 --> 00:50:11.580
relying on.

00:50:11.580 --> 00:50:13.260
But what detail did he overlook?

00:50:14.060 --> 00:50:15.580
What detail did Black overlook?

00:50:16.460 --> 00:50:17.820
No, not knight takes f7.

00:50:17.820 --> 00:50:20.380
Knight f7, you can actually even take the bishop with check.

00:50:21.180 --> 00:50:23.100
And Black is up two minor pieces.

00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:24.940
It's not knight takes f7.

00:50:24.940 --> 00:50:25.980
It's f takes g3.

00:50:26.540 --> 00:50:29.580
It's f takes g3, which taking away from the center

00:50:29.580 --> 00:50:30.780
is a move that's often missed.

00:50:31.500 --> 00:50:32.860
And look at the bishop on f5.

00:50:32.860 --> 00:50:37.820
It's hanging, so Black not only loses a pawn, but his entire kingside is also shattered.

00:50:37.820 --> 00:50:42.620
So f takes g3 is what Black missed, and White won the game, as you can see by the 1-0.

00:50:43.420 --> 00:50:45.580
Okay, so that's one example.

00:50:46.380 --> 00:50:50.140
Let me see if I can quickly find another where Black, in fact, gets checkmated.

00:50:52.300 --> 00:50:55.980
Okay, here's one where Black eventually…

00:50:57.340 --> 00:50:59.180
Oh, this is a sick game, actually.

00:50:59.180 --> 00:50:59.820
Just a moment.

00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:04.460
Just a moment.

00:51:04.460 --> 00:51:05.020
Setting it up.

00:51:07.340 --> 00:51:11.980
Yeah, I can see the same blunder being committed very, very frequently with f takes g3.

00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:21.180
Okay, this is super nice.

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:23.340
This is super, super nice.

00:51:23.340 --> 00:51:25.180
And I'm not totally sure that this was sound.

00:51:25.740 --> 00:51:27.180
So very similar position.

00:51:27.980 --> 00:51:28.700
Just a second.

00:51:30.140 --> 00:51:31.500
Very similar position.

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:35.260
And then I'll show the third game that I wanted to illustrate an important concept.

00:51:36.300 --> 00:51:37.500
Okay, here we go.

00:51:38.220 --> 00:51:39.340
This is not a well-known game.

00:51:39.340 --> 00:51:41.180
I don't know these players, but it doesn't matter.

00:51:41.180 --> 00:51:43.660
The concepts are more important.

00:51:43.660 --> 00:51:48.140
So in this position, Black plays knight h5, okay?

00:51:50.540 --> 00:51:53.420
And obviously, as you know, White plays knight takes g5.

00:51:53.420 --> 00:51:55.420
Black plays knight takes g3.

00:51:55.420 --> 00:51:57.580
And Black does not have a bishop on f5.

00:51:57.580 --> 00:52:01.820
The problem here is this intermediate move, bishop h7 check.

00:52:01.820 --> 00:52:02.780
This is a brilliant move.

00:52:03.820 --> 00:52:05.660
King h8, take the knight.

00:52:05.660 --> 00:52:08.700
Notice that White takes with the f-pawn anyway to open up the rook.

00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:09.820
Now the bishop drops back.

00:52:09.820 --> 00:52:10.940
What's the problem?

00:52:10.940 --> 00:52:13.340
The problem is that White threatens mate in 2.

00:52:13.340 --> 00:52:15.820
And I'm sure that Black was relying on the move knight f6.

00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:18.620
But simply rook takes f6, and the game is over.

00:52:19.260 --> 00:52:21.660
Queen h5 and queen g7 is checkmate.

00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:23.900
And queen h7, that is.

00:52:23.900 --> 00:52:28.140
So instead, Black plays bishop h6 to open up a running square for the king.

00:52:29.020 --> 00:52:31.100
But White brings the knight into the attack.

00:52:31.100 --> 00:52:32.780
And then the queen into the attack.

00:52:32.780 --> 00:52:35.900
And White finds a sexy crushing move in this position.

00:52:35.900 --> 00:52:37.180
Who can spot it?

00:52:37.180 --> 00:52:38.460
I don't even think it's the best move.

00:52:38.460 --> 00:52:40.540
I think White had a move for that one immediately.

00:52:40.540 --> 00:52:42.060
But the concept was correct.

00:52:42.940 --> 00:52:45.020
Yeah, well, knight takes d6 is what White plays.

00:52:45.580 --> 00:52:47.820
I think knight takes g5 is even stronger.

00:52:48.780 --> 00:52:50.220
White played knight takes d6.

00:52:50.220 --> 00:52:51.500
Same concept everywhere.

00:52:51.580 --> 00:52:54.620
If Black takes, then you obviously just get checkmated.

00:52:56.300 --> 00:52:59.340
But knight g5 also threatens a fork on e6.

00:53:00.220 --> 00:53:01.420
So Black just can't.

00:53:01.420 --> 00:53:02.220
It's overwhelming.

00:53:02.220 --> 00:53:05.180
After knight d6, Black could stave off defeat for a couple more moves.

